
Description:

MDN/parse (RFC 9007) is implemented.  It was a stub that answered
notParsable for every blob.

MDN/send now issues a well-formed null envelope sender.  It used to send
"MAIL FROM:<<>>", which SMTP servers reject.

MDN/send follows RFC 9007, Section 2.1: it takes the identityId and
onSuccessUpdateEmail arguments, and requires the latter to set the
$mdnsent keyword on each message, rather than setting the keyword
itself.  A client may name its own address as finalRecipient, and may add
extensionFields.  Properties RFC 9007 does not define, or defines as
server-set, are rejected.  A missing message or one without a
Disposition-Notification-To header is reported as notFound, the error
RFC 9007 assigns, instead of emailNotFound or noRecipients.

MDN/send accepts the lowercase Disposition values of RFC 9007, such as
"mdn-sent-manually", as well as the RFC 8098 capitalisation it used to
require.  The Original-Recipient field it writes is now well-formed.

MDN/parse decodes the subject and text body, lowercases the Disposition
values, keeps the address-type on recipient fields, and reports
extensionFields.

The MDN that the Sieve "reject" action sends had a doubled address-type
in Original-Recipient when sieve_mdn_original_recipient_header named a
header that already carries one, which its default does.


Documentation:

None.  The behaviour follows the cited specification as written.


Config changes:

None.


Upgrade instructions:

Clients written against the earlier MDN/send must now pass identityId
(the value Identity/get reports) and an onSuccessUpdateEmail argument
that sets the $mdnsent keyword, as RFC 9007 requires.


